outline: the studio's goal, built in Go. One tree from the root, read once
and drawn twice: on the terminal, and as a page in the browser. Taken:
studio, the frame, hosts fm and ed as they are
(`todo/studio/outline:/The frame: studio hosts/`); it is the one new
piece, for a frame fits none of the others
(`todo/studio/principles:/Built on sys, not beside it/`).

The shape, as built: pieces that pass messages, not one program
  fm, ed     as they were, each a tangle tree, drawing through sys/outline,
             and on the terminal through sys/view
  studio     the frame, a tangle tree too: 1-frame 2-term 3-page 4-lens
             5-verbs 6-main
  packages   outline, tty, view, watch and state, and figure, the one
             primitive built so far

The structure is the tree, not a language
  Folders and files carry it down to the idea, and inside a file the
  note's own outline carries the rest: its indent, items and labels. A
  file per idea, not per field: field files cost a file per label, make a
  task slower to write, and change nothing on the page. An s-expression is
  only a way to print the tree for a program, as `medium/page/list` --json
  does; no page language sits between the tree and a view.

A primitive is a kind the views know: the text it matches, such as !290,
or a whole note, as a figure is; how the terminal draws it, how the page
draws it, and what can be done with it. It is a package of its own, as
sys/figure is, and a view asks it by kind: no plugin system and no
runtime library, as a page already carries its parts
(`todo/studio/studio:/carries its parts by/`). A primitive earns its place
when the notes keep needing what the reader cannot say, and it replaces a
program, or two views need it.

The terminal is one view, behind an abstraction
  fm does what a file manager does, and ed what an editor does; how either
  is shown is abstracted, in sys/outline. A program says what to show as
  nodes, the same Node as the outline: fm's rows, ed's lines and
  selections. It takes keys by name. A view draws the nodes and hands back
  the names:

    type View interface {
        Draw(n *Node, status string, state []string) // the tree, the status
        Key() (Key, bool)                            // what was pressed, where
        Ask(prompt, def string) (string, bool)       // a line typed in answer
        Size() (w, h int)                            // how much shows at once
        Copy(text string)                            // text to the clipboard
    }

  Any system that implements View can show and work fm and ed: the
  terminal on tty, a page, wish. Nodes, not cells: cells would make the
  page a terminal in a browser, as ttyd does, where nodes let it draw a
  pill or a chip. Interaction is the other half of a view: a key comes back
  by name, with the node it was said on, as a click names one, and the
  text a paste brings; a line typed at a prompt comes back through Ask.
  The window is the view's own, so the terminal scrolls by itself and a
  page would scroll natively.
  fm and ed draw, take their keys, ask and copy through View, their screens
  as they were. Still on tty directly: fm's title and the
  suspend around a spawned program or an fzf picker, and ed's check for
  keys still waiting. The keys . puts back to replay a change wait in ed's
  own queue, ahead of the view's.
  A page view still runs fm or ed here, as a process beside the files: the
  page is its screen and its keys, over localhost with a token. That is
  the step studio puts off (`todo/studio/studio:/It makes the page an app/`),
  and this goal takes it.

Messages, not calls
  A foundation of all of sys, as the outline is:
  `README.md:/everything talks in messages/`. A program and its view pass
  only plain data: in one process a call whose arguments are data; down a
  pipe or a socket a JSON line each, as outline.Stream writes them. fm -w
  and ed -w run the program with its view in another process,
  `sh/window`, which Tk draws and outline.Window starts. It draws ed's
  text as text, in the terminal's font, and hands ed every key.
    to the view      draw: the tree, the program's word and its state;
                     ask: a prompt; copy: text for the clipboard
    to the program   key: its name, the node it was said on, the rune a
                     click fell on, and any text; answer: the line
                     typed; size: how much the view shows
  A message joins when a view needs one, as a primitive does. ed sends
  every line, until a measure says lines must be read on demand. What only
  a terminal can do stays with it: in a window fm says so of a picker,
  help, or a program run in its place; o and e open beside it, and run.

The frame: studio hosts the pieces
  studio is the frame, a tangle tree of its own; fm and ed stay as they
  are. The frame is a note, `studio/frame`: its lines stack down, the
  lines under one go across, and so on, turn about. Each line is a slot,
  a pane or a split into slots; a share such as 40% is its part of its
  parent, and the rest of the line is what runs in it.
  Each slot is a process that speaks messages, so composing is the design
  and not an accident of where it runs:
    fm -s, ed -s  their view at the other end of stdin and stdout, the
                  same JSON lines fm -w sends `sh/window`
    ed -f         the thing at cur as written, and editable: source is
                  ed, chasing cur; its lines are nodes named path:line
    lens          a slot a frame may add: the views derived from the
                  thing, outline, diff, figure and run, read-only
    shell         the one slot that is a terminal: tmux in control mode
                  runs it, and studio draws its screen in the slot
  studio draws each slot's nodes in its region, keeps which slot has the
  eye, and hands it the keys; a click goes to the slot under it, as a key
  on the node there. The same note lays out on a page, each slot the
  page's view of the same process, and in Tk. tmux is not the frame: it
  runs the shell, and it is where studio was started, if it was.

The socket: studio's words are tmux's, for slots
  Programmable as tmux is: studio listens on a socket. list, the slots and
  what runs in each; send, keys or text into one; read, its nodes, not a
  scrape of its screen; focus; lens; and watch, a line for each thing that
  happens, a draw, the eye moving, cur, as tmux -C streams its
  notifications. studio send shell make is one line from any program, an
  agent's too. Until the socket, send, read and lens are studio's verbs,
  which tmux carries out.

The look
  The top line says where cur is; over the tree its layouts, folder, tree
  and tiles, the one in use lit; beside it the thing itself, in ed; and in
  a lens slot, when a frame has one, the derived views as tabs, each with
  its number and its tally, a lens not built dim. What the cursor is on is
  lit across its whole row, with a blue edge, and the scaffolding is dim,
  so that names are the loudest thing. As tiles a folder is cards, each a
  name, its kind and its facts. The palette is the page's tokens, dark or
  light. None of it is built: sys/view draws fm's look today.

Who holds what
  program  the data, the cursor and the selection: what its verbs act on.
           fm sends one tree, from its top, where it began, down through
           what a view opened, folders and text files, and the way to the
           cursor, which may be on an item inside a file; going out past
           the top brings the top along.
  view     how it shows, in one of three layouts, t turning to the next:
           the cursor's folder as a list, the tree from the top, or the
           folder as tiles, a grid read left to right. It moves the cursor
           among what it shows, down, up, to the last, a page, and in the
           terminal to a row by its number; as tiles right and left move
           too, and down and up go a line of tiles. It tells the program
           where the cursor landed. In the tree it goes in and out itself:
           a node opens or closes in place, the view saying open or close
           on it and the program sending what is inside, and out goes up to
           the node a row is in. Its hints are its own: in the terminal a
           dim ▸ on what is shut with something inside, and nothing on what
           is open, for its kids under it say so; depth by the indent alone,
           and a bracket, ╭ to ╰, down a text file's items, which the window
           tints instead. In the other two, going in and out stays the
           program's, for it changes what is shown, and so does g, the
           first file, which skips the folder's own . and .. rows that only
           fm knows.
  between  ideas, by name: down, in, zoom, rename. A key is one way to say
           one and a click another. A view does the ideas of moving itself,
           and hands the rest to the program with the node they were said
           on. ed passes its keys through: an editor's keys are its
           language.

What opening shows, and what a key does, go by kind
  Opening a node shows what its kind holds: a folder its rows, a text file
  its items, anything else nothing, for o opens it beside. That is a
  format dispatcher in place, `fm/4-main/35-inside.go`, and a new format
  joins it as one more case. Every text file is read the same way, by
  outline.Text: by its indent, as Python is read, a line's kids being the
  lines indented under it, and a line that hangs under the text after a
  dash, a number or a label going on with the line above. No markup and
  no guessing; text with no structure is made structured by indenting it.
  An item's id is its path and line, an address, so e edits there.
  A key goes by kind too: the view's own first, moving and folding, then
  what the cursor is on. On a file's row it is fm's file verbs; on an item
  an item's, in, out and e, so no file verb reaches a file through one of
  its items.

The status line, at the bottom only, in every view
  On the left the program's word, what just happened or what it asks; on
  the right the state, a few short words from whoever holds each: the
  program's, [2] selected, cut:3, mark:x, dotfiles, the search; then the
  view's, its layout and where the cursor is, 12/34. Short of room the
  word is cut, for the state says what holds now. The top line keeps
  where: the path, or an item's address. A prompt takes the bottom line
  while it asks. ed says its mode, + while unsaved, and held while that
  keeps it from cur's file; its views add where the cursor is, line:col.

fm and ed as primitives, three ways
  taken     studio hosts fm and ed, each a process as it is, its view
            at the other end of messages
            (`todo/studio/outline:/The frame: studio hosts/`). fm does
            not grow to hold the others, as was proposed. ed stays ed:
            studio never edits text itself, and e hands ed the item, in
            the terminal or on the page.
  later     fm and ed as Go packages, each a component with its keys and
            its views, their mains ten lines, so one process can host
            both on one page. A primitive is a Go interface from the
            start, so this stays open.
  not       both stitched into one main.go: each has its own main, the
            two share the top-level names binding and userHome, and
            tangle walks with WalkDir, which does not follow a linked
            folder.

The check: `outline/text_test.go` carries one note through every rule,
and over every text file in sys the nodes say, word for word and in
order, what the file says.

What retires once it holds: page/todo and page/list, the python that reads
and draws notes (`todo/studio/principles:/A piece that serves/`).

Open
  - fm and ed on one page from one process, or a page each
  - select moves down within the cursor's folder only, so in the tree it
    stops at the folder's last row
  - a file is gone into only in the tree: in the folder and as tiles,
    enter on a file's row edits, until the keys are settled
  - an item's id is its line, so an edit above it moves it; the cursor
    then goes back to the file's row
  - a text file is read on every key, so one over a megabyte stays shut
  - fm's yank still runs xclip; the view's copy would carry it into a
    window too
  - ed in the window: a click does not move the cursor, for the window
    sends no click on ed's text
  - in the window the lens's kinds are drawn plain, and a figure as its
    items: the window knows fm's kinds alone

The plan, each step checked to change nothing it should not
  1  fm -s and ed -s: the view at the other end of stdin and stdout.
     Built.
  2  fm's terminal view lifted out of fm into sys/view, beside outline,
     drawing on the whole terminal or in a region a host gives it. It
     stayed in fm while fm was the one program that drew an outline on
     a terminal; studio is the second, which lifts it
     (`programming/standalone:/no second reader/`). Built: fm's screens
     as they were, in all three layouts, until step 3 changed the tree's
     hint; a test holds the three (`view/view_test.go:/func TestLayouts/`).
  3  the lens sends nodes, not escape codes. Built: studio show is a
     program as fm is, its view sys/view on its pane, Tk with -w, where
     its kinds are plain (Open), or a host's with -s; a line is a node
     named path:line. The tree's one hint is a dim ▸ on what is shut. And
     figure is a lens, not a program: sys/figure reads a note and lays it
     out, in runes and as SVG, a test holding each
     (`figure/figure_test.go:/func TestSVG/`); view draws a node of kind
     figure in runes, and studio figure writes the page.
  4  source is ed: the thing at cur is edited where it shows. Its source
     is typed at rest, so ed edits it; outline, diff, figure and run are
     derived in motion, so they stay lenses, read-only
     (`todo/studio/aggregate:/typed at rest/`). Built: ed -f chases cur
     in a slot, another file opening at cur's line, the same file moving
     only the cursor, and an unsaved buffer holding, saying so, until a
     save lets it go; ed's lines named path:line, ready for a click,
     which no view sends yet; the frame fm and ed over the shell;
     sys/watch, which the lens and ed share. Then: ed's terminal view
     lifted out of ed, as fm's was, so a host draws it; the view then
     hands a node of kind text every key, for an editor's keys are its
     language.
  5  studio hosts the slots: it starts each with pipes, draws each in
     its region, and hands the eye, the keys and the clicks to the right
     one. Ready for it: a stream starts without the eye; tty reads clicks
     and Ctrl chords; view knows the node at each cell, and figure the
     part (view.At, figure.At); a key carries the rune a click fell on.
     Still to do: a prompt in a region, for Ask asks on the terminal's
     bottom line, and a row's number typed to a host's view.
  6  studio's socket: list, send, read, focus, lens, watch.
  7  the shell through tmux's control mode. Until then the shell is a
     tmux pane, as studio lays the frame out today.
  8  a page view of fm and ed: the page hosts the same slots.
  9  the keys, decided last, once the ideas are settled. The keys
     review left these to decide here:
       ideas      each binding names its idea beside its key, so a key, a
                  click and a button say one thing, and the wire carries
                  ideas; a click's landing says select, which fm logs
                  through c as p -c does, where a move only says it to
                  cur
       vi         digits a count, 3j, and a row by :N or NG; g first and
                  G last everywhere, a count before G its row; l in, as
                  right is, and link to another letter
       m, t, q    m is memory in fm and the bracket in ed, so the next
                  lens moves to gt and gT; t is till in ed, so the layout
                  turns by another key, and never in a figure or a text
                  node; q in a slot ends its program, or the frame closes
                  slots, as vim's Ctrl-W q does
       the frame  Ctrl-W, vim's window prefix, which no slot binds: h j k
                  l move the eye, : opens the frame's words, q closes a
                  slot; the shell's word erase comes back as Ctrl-W .;
                  on a page the browser keeps Ctrl-W, and a click moves
                  the eye
       one table  keys and gestures to ideas, read by view, `sh/window`,
                  the page and `tmux/conf`, and listed by ?; today six
                  places decide keys, and the two copies of the view's
                  disagree on digits
       the word   one function finds the word at a column, as tmux and p
                  do, and p takes -w dir, for a program's own folder
       text in    p -a and studio send paste with brackets, not typed
                  keys, so that fm and ed take text and not commands
       chords     tmux hands clicks through, but a chord only as one
                  button's press and the other's release: acme's chords
                  are read from that, or traded for keys
       sweeps     a drag's end beside its start, once a view sends one
